88
00:07:31,230 --> 00:07:33,250
 If we go back to that, let's start simple.

89
00:07:34,040 --> 00:07:40,900
 A database is basically a place where computer keeps information in an organized way.

90
00:07:41,020 --> 00:07:48,600
 So think of it like a giant, super tidy cupboard, if you want to think of it like that.

91
00:07:50,320 --> 00:07:52,720
 So everything has its own shelf.

92
00:07:54,640 --> 00:07:57,560
 And you can always find it again.

93
00:07:59,410 --> 00:08:04,220
 The information, of course, stays there, even when the computer is turned off.

94
00:08:05,830 --> 00:08:10,280
 So every app on your phone has basically a database behind it.

95
00:08:11,200 --> 00:08:16,890
 So for example, if you open up Spotify, it looks up your playlists in a database, for example.

96
00:08:17,390 --> 00:08:22,600
 Or when you log in with BankID, a database checks who you are.

97
00:08:27,000 --> 00:08:32,570
 So why not use Excel?

98
00:08:33,050 --> 00:08:36,140
 I mean, Excel is great, but it has limits.

99
00:08:37,730 --> 00:08:41,540
 I mean, if you have a million of rows, it gets pretty slow.

100
00:08:42,620 --> 00:08:48,270
 For example, if 10 people edit the same file, it becomes basically a mess.

101
00:08:48,270 --> 00:08:59,890
 And in Excel, you can type a phone number in the price column, for example, and nobody would

102
00:08:59,890 --> 00:09:00,470
 stop you.

103
00:09:01,310 --> 00:09:05,240
 But the database has rules, so wrong data can't get in.

104
00:09:07,470 --> 00:09:12,390
 And to find something, you just ask the question in basically one line.

105
00:09:18,950 --> 00:09:24,750
 So table, you can see a table is like class list.

106
00:09:25,360 --> 00:09:30,250
 I mean, a database is made of tables.

107
00:09:30,740 --> 00:09:35,880
 So think of a class list your teacher had in school, okay?

108
00:09:37,000 --> 00:09:40,550
 And one list for one class.

109
00:09:41,200 --> 00:09:47,400
 And each table in a database is about one kind of a thing.

110
00:09:48,390 --> 00:09:53,190
 So here we have, for example, table of customers.

111
00:09:54,030 --> 00:09:55,760
 Another table could be products.

112
00:09:56,360 --> 00:09:58,000
 And another could be orders.

113
00:09:59,320 --> 00:10:02,940
 So we keep different things in different tables, basically.

114
00:10:06,360 --> 00:10:10,320
 Sorry, need to take a sip of coffee here.

115
00:10:10,320 --> 00:10:14,680
 Rows and columns, two important words.

116
00:10:16,200 --> 00:10:18,420
 A row is one thing.

117
00:10:20,660 --> 00:10:25,050
 And also, one row is one customer.

118
00:10:25,230 --> 00:10:28,340
 For example, Anna here that we have.

119
00:10:30,710 --> 00:10:35,790
 Column is one piece of information that every row has, like a city, for example.

120
00:10:36,880 --> 00:10:43,180
 A good way to remember it is basically a row is one person.

121
00:10:43,820 --> 00:10:48,410
 Column is one question you ask about everyone.

122
00:10:48,830 --> 00:10:50,480
 So what's your city, for example?

123
00:10:51,520 --> 00:10:56,080
 Every customer answers in the same column.

124
00:11:05,390 --> 00:11:06,330
 Okay, data types.

125
00:11:06,750 --> 00:11:09,540
 So every column has a data type.

126
00:11:10,760 --> 00:11:13,410
 That's the kind of information it holds.

127
00:11:13,770 --> 00:11:16,610
 For example, an integer is a whole number.

128
00:11:20,460 --> 00:11:25,240
 Real here is a number with decimals, like prices.

129
00:11:26,380 --> 00:11:31,580
 And text, in this case, is words.

130
00:11:32,770 --> 00:11:35,170
 And null is special.

131
00:11:36,290 --> 00:11:38,250
 Null means we don't know, basically.

132
00:11:39,020 --> 00:11:41,830
 It's not zero and it's not empty text.

133
00:11:42,070 --> 00:11:43,330
